      "content": "<p>Consider the following statements :</p>\n<p>(A) The principal quantum number 'n' is a positive integer with values of 'n' = 1, 2, 3, ...</p>\n<p>(B) The azimuthal quantum number 'l' for a given 'n' (principal quantum number) can have values as 'l' = 0, 1, 2, ...... n</p>\n<p>(C) Magnetic orbital quantum number 'm<sub>l</sub>' for a particular 'l' (azimuthal quantum number) has (2l + 1) values.</p>\n<p>(D) <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.507ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -666 778 666\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"B1\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-B1\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> 1/2 are the two possible orientations of electron spin.</p>\n<p>(E) For l = 5, there will be a total of 9 orbital</p>\n<p>Which of the above statements are correct?</p>",
      "options": [
